From ef23304072c84b2ca6c5533e9039ccc85c6bbdd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:08:26 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] dsv41: TF_DS_FUSED_ROPE_STORE (off by default): the
 norm-RoPE-store glue of the kv-source and index-source layers in one launch
 each

TF_DS_FUSED_ROPE_STORE gates five glue chains, off by default: the decode indexer key (the kit's
`_rmsnorm` + `_rope_heads` + `_fp4_store`), the decode latent (`_rope_heads` + `_fp4_store`), the
decode indexer query (`_rope_heads` + `_fp4qd_p2`), and the prompt indexer key / latent (torch's
rms_norm and rope arithmetic + the kit's `_fp4_store`). Each call site keeps exactly the arithmetic
it launches today and gates on fuseOk: a shape the fused kernels do not take runs the unfused
launches instead (never a hard error). The decode path replicates the Triton kernels (including
`_rmsnorm`'s tl.sum reduction order, reconstructed as in TF_DS_FUSED_COMPRESS2_NORM); the prompt
path reuses the already-torch-parity Exact kernels. `_rope_heads`/`_fp4_store`/`_fp4qd_p2` are
elementwise (max is exact) and bit-safe to replicate; `_rmsnorm` and the Triton fma contraction in
`_rope_heads` are the honest hardware-side risks. The E4M3 scale encoder keeps the Triton source's
saturation arm (`fb >= (1087 << 20)`, i.e. bits(480.0f), so f >= 480): without it the normal path
runs past 0x7F and f >= 480 encodes as 0x80 (a negative-zero scale) where the kit saturates to 448 -
the CPU harness caught this (22,161 blocks) and the host test now pins amax 2879, 2880 and 1e4.
`tf_ds_torch_rmsnorm_row`'s shared reduction array is 512 lanes (rmsLanes is up to 512 for d = 2048
at <= 3 rows; it was 128). Off, the unfused launches run byte for byte. Engram's add2Bf16 +
`_engram_gate` is deliberately left unfused (three tl.sum reductions for two launches a pass).
UNVERIFIED-BUILD on this machine (no nvcc): gated on the exact suite on a Spark like the rest of the
series. Per-round saving is ~20 launches (4 kv-source layers x 3, 8 index-source layers x 1), i.e.
roughly 0.1-0.2% of a round, and is only worth keeping if TF_DS_PDL shows real device-side gaps.
